{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.HookSumRegisterBridge"],"declarations":60,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.pos_of_check_and_tail","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.pos_of_check_and_tail","kind":"theorem","line":1403,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L1403-L1415","doc":"**A finite exact check plus a crude tail bound gives positivity throughout.**\n\nThis is the exact logical shape of `qou-k5r5` claim (b) once weakened to (b′).\n`hfin` is the exactly-computable part (`n₀ ≤ n ≤ N`, done here to `N = 42`);\n`htail` is the only analytic input, and it is the crude form. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.pos_of_abs_sub_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.pos_of_abs_sub_lt","kind":"theorem","line":1394,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L1394-L1401","doc":"**Positivity from a crude approximation.** If `x` is within `k` of `k` then\n`x > 0`. No positivity hypothesis on `k` is needed — `|x - k| < k` already\nforces `0 < k`, since the left side is non-negative. It is the whole of the\nweakening: an effective bound need only be accurate to within the limit's own\nsize. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.cov_eq_cov_symmetrised","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.cov_eq_cov_symmetrised","kind":"theorem","line":1348,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L1348-L1364","doc":"The symmetric-part form: averaging `X` with `X ∘ T` changes nothing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.cov_involution","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.cov_involution","kind":"theorem","line":1326,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L1326-L1346","doc":"**Only the `T`-symmetric part of `X` contributes.** If an involution `T`\npreserves the weight and fixes `Y`, then `X` may be replaced by `X ∘ T`.\n\nThis is the step asserted in `wlambda_covariance_epic_M1_kappa_structure.py`:\nPlancherel is conjugation-invariant and `∑ c²` is conjugation-fixed, so\n`Cov(∑ λᵢ², ∑ c²) = Cov(∑ (λ'ⱼ)², ∑ c²)` and hence, by\n`two_mul_sum_hookLen`, both equal `Cov(∑ h, ∑ c²)`. The antisymmetric part of\n`∑ λᵢ²` — which is `∑ c`, by the companion identity — contributes nothing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.cov_pos_iff_corr_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.cov_pos_iff_corr_pos","kind":"theorem","line":1312,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L1312-L1324","doc":"**The sign of a covariance is the sign of the correlation.** Trivial, and\nit is the whole of the `kappa > 0` reduction: the two normalising factors are\nstandard deviations, so they cannot change the sign. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.cov_add_const","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.cov_add_const","kind":"theorem","line":1294,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L1294-L1310","doc":"**Covariance ignores a deterministic shift.** With `Sum h^2 = Sum c^2 + n^2`\nand `n` deterministic, this is exactly the step\n`Cov(Sum h, Sum c^2) = Cov(Sum h, Sum h^2)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.cov","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.cov","kind":"def","line":1289,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L1289-L1292","doc":"Covariance of two observables under a finite weight. Stated for a weight\nthat sums to `1`, which is how the edge measure is normalised. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_lt_of_split_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_lt_of_split_bound","kind":"theorem","line":1238,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L1238-L1253","doc":"The same reduction with the two edge classes separated, which is the shape\nthe route actually has: same-sign edges are bounded by the DENSITY reading, and\nopposite-sign ones by the refined telescoping bound. `S` is the same-sign class. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_lt_of_weighted_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_lt_of_weighted_bound","kind":"theorem","line":1225,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L1225-L1236","doc":"**The reduction, with the analytic input isolated.** If every edge's ratio is\nbounded by `b e`, and the weights are nonnegative, and the BOUNDS' weighted total\nfalls short of the total weight, then so does the ratio's — i.e. `N < P`, i.e.\n`M₁ > 0`. Everything except `hagg` is per-edge and already proved. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_hookLen_eq_diag","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_hookLen_eq_diag","kind":"theorem","line":1153,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L1153-L1182","doc":"**The diagonal form of the hook sum.**\n\n    ∑_cells h  =  ∑_{c ≥ 0} (D c² + c · D c)  +  ∑_{r ≥ 1} (Dᵀ r² + r · Dᵀ r)\n\ni.e. `∑ h = ∑_c D(c)² + ∑_c |c| D(c)` over all integer diagonals. Combined with\n`sum_hookLen_eq_sum_coords`, this is the exact statement behind the `|u|`\ncancellation that makes `∑ h` fluctuate at order `n` rather than `n^{3/2}` —\nsee the section header. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_cells_lower_eq_sum_diag","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_cells_lower_eq_sum_diag","kind":"theorem","line":1111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L1111-L1151","doc":"The mirror of `sum_cells_upper_eq_sum_diag` below the main diagonal: cell\n`k` of transpose-diagonal `r ≥ 1` is `(k + r, k)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_cells_upper_eq_sum_diag","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_cells_upper_eq_sum_diag","kind":"theorem","line":1056,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L1056-L1091","doc":"Summing over the cells on or above the main diagonal, grouped by diagonal.\nCell `k` of diagonal `c` is `(k, k + c)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_hookLen_eq_sum_coords","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_hookLen_eq_sum_coords","kind":"theorem","line":978,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L978-L1028","doc":"**`∑_cells h = ∑_cells (i + j + 1)`.**\n\nThe hook sum equals the sum of the \"anti-diagonal\" coordinate. This is the\nsingle-diagram form of the growth-chain step used in\n`wlambda_covariance_epic_M1_kappa_structure.py`: adding a box at `(r, s)` raises\n`∑ h` by exactly `1 + r + s`, because the new cell has hook `1` and every cell\nleft of it in its row and above it in its column gains exactly one. Summing that\nover a standard Young tableau gives a path sum that must be path-INDEPENDENT —\nand this is why: it is just `∑_cells (i + j + 1)` of the final shape.\n\nIt implies `two_mul_sum_hookLen`, since `∑ i = n(λ)` and `∑ j = n(λ')`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.two_mul_sum_hookLen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.two_mul_sum_hookLen","kind":"theorem","line":887,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L887-L956","doc":"**The hook sum is the conjugation-symmetric combination.**\n\n    2 * Sum_cells h = Sum_i (rowLen i)² + Sum_j (colLen j)²\n\nRow `i` contributes `rowLen i (rowLen i + 1)/2` of arm, column `j` contributes\n`colLen j (colLen j - 1)/2` of leg; the two linear terms are each `n` and cancel.\nEquivalently `Sum h = (Sum λᵢ² + Sum λ'ⱼ²)/2`, which is the statement measured\nas ~96 % anti-correlated in the probe. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.hookLen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.hookLen","kind":"def","line":863,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L863-L865","doc":"The hook length of a cell: arm + leg + 1, written so both `ℕ` subtractions\nare valid on the diagram (`j < rowLen i` and `i < colLen j` there). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.colLen_eq_zero_of_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.colLen_eq_zero_of_le","kind":"theorem","line":855,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L855-L861","doc":"Columns past `λ₁` are empty. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_cells_transpose","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_cells_transpose","kind":"theorem","line":844,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L844-L853","doc":"Transposing swaps the coordinates of every cell. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_cells_eq_sum_rows","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_cells_eq_sum_rows","kind":"theorem","line":822,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L822-L842","doc":"Summing over the diagram, grouped by rows. Any `C` past the last nonempty\nrow works. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.two_mul_areaUpper_le_card_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.two_mul_areaUpper_le_card_iff","kind":"theorem","line":790,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L790-L803","doc":"**`2 A₊ ≤ n` is exactly \"the dipole moment is bounded by the Durfee side\".**\n\nStated as an `iff` deliberately: the left side is FALSE pointwise — the one-row\ndiagram `(n)` has `A₊ = n - 1`, `A₋ = 0`, `D 0 = 1` — so `A_σ ≤ n/2` can only\never hold *in expectation*, and any route that needs it must average. This is\nthe reformulation that says what the average has to control: not an area, but\nthe imbalance between the two halves. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.areaLower_transpose","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.areaLower_transpose","kind":"theorem","line":785,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L785-L788","doc":"The other direction, via `μᵀᵀ = μ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.areaUpper_transpose","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.areaUpper_transpose","kind":"theorem","line":779,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L779-L783","doc":"Conjugation swaps the two areas. Under any conjugation-invariant measure —\nPlancherel is one — this is what forces `E[A₊] = E[A₋]`, hence\n`E[A_σ] = (n - E[D 0])/2 < n/2` unconditionally. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.areaUpper_add_areaLower_add_diagLen_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.areaUpper_add_areaLower_add_diagLen_zero","kind":"theorem","line":761,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L761-L777","doc":"**The exact area split.** `A₊ + A₋ + D 0 = n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.areaLower","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.areaLower","kind":"def","line":758,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L758-L759","doc":"Area strictly below the main diagonal: cells of content `< 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.areaUpper","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.areaUpper","kind":"def","line":755,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L755-L756","doc":"Area strictly above the main diagonal: cells of content `> 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_diagLen_add_transpose","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_diagLen_add_transpose","kind":"theorem","line":734,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L734-L745","doc":"The symmetric form: sweeping both ways double-counts the main diagonal. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.card_eq_sum_diagLen_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.card_eq_sum_diagLen_add","kind":"theorem","line":725,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L725-L732","doc":"**The diagonal decomposition.** Every cell is on exactly one diagonal of `μ`\n(content `≥ 0`) or one diagonal `r ≥ 1` of `μᵀ` (content `< 0`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_diagLen_eq_card_lower","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_diagLen_eq_card_lower","kind":"theorem","line":688,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L688-L723","doc":"The lower half — cells strictly below the main diagonal — swept by diagonals\n`r ≥ 1` of the transpose. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_diagLen_eq_card_upper","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_diagLen_eq_card_upper","kind":"theorem","line":654,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L654-L686","doc":"The upper half — cells on or above the main diagonal — swept by diagonals of\n`μ`. Any `T` past `λ₁` works, since `diagLen μ c = 0` there. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_drop_diagLen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_drop_diagLen","kind":"theorem","line":623,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L623-L632","doc":"**The telescoping total for a Young diagram.** The profile falls from `D 0`\nto `0` across `[0, λ₁)`, so the sum of the one-step drops is exactly `D 0` — the\nDurfee side — and the diagram's global drop density is exactly `D 0 / λ₁`. This\nis the quantity the `M₁ > 0` route trades the effective CLT for: it is an\nidentity, not an estimate. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_rowLen_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_rowLen_zero","kind":"theorem","line":614,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L614-L621","doc":"Past the first row every diagonal is empty: `i + λ₁ < rowLen i ≤ λ₁` is\nimpossible. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.kappa_uniform_dot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.kappa_uniform_dot","kind":"theorem","line":599,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L599-L610","doc":"**Uniform κ gives exactly the global density.** If every site carries the\nsame weight, the ω-average of the interval densities collapses to the total fall\nof `D` spread over all `T` sites. So `infl = 1` is precisely the uniform case,\nand the inflation factor measures nothing but κ's departure from uniform. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.weighted_density_eq_kappa_dot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.weighted_density_eq_kappa_dot","kind":"theorem","line":569,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L569-L597","doc":"**The weighted average of interval densities is the κ-average of `δ`.**\nEach interval's density `(D lo − D hi)/(hi − lo)` is the mean of the one-step\ndrops over its sites; weighting and exchanging the two sums replaces the\nintervals by κ. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_drop_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_drop_eq","kind":"theorem","line":559,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L559-L567","doc":"Telescoping over an interval: the total fall of `D` across `[lo, hi)` is\n`D lo − D hi`, whatever `D` is. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.kappa_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.kappa_sum","kind":"theorem","line":541,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L541-L557","doc":"**κ is a distribution on sites.** Each interval spreads exactly its own\nweight, so the site weights sum to the total interval weight. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.kappa","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.kappa","kind":"def","line":535,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L535-L539","doc":"`κ t` — the weight a finite family of intervals `[lo e, hi e)` puts on site\n`t`, each interval spreading its weight `w e` uniformly over the sites it\ncovers. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_sub_transpose_le_refined","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_sub_transpose_le_refined","kind":"theorem","line":457,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L457-L475","doc":"**The refined opposite-sign bound.** For an edge whose near corner sits at\ndistance `a` on `μ`'s side and whose far corner sits at distance `r` on `μᵀ`'s,\n\n    D_μ a − D_μᵀ r  ≤  min (D 0) (λ₁ − a)  −  max 0 (D 0 − r).\n\nThe first term bounds the near diagonal both by the main diagonal and by how much\nroom is left in row `0`; the second says the far diagonal cannot have lost more\nthan `r` of its `D 0` cells. Dropping both refinements gives back\n`abs_diagLen_sub_transpose_le`'s `D 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_add_le_rowLen_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_add_le_rowLen_zero","kind":"theorem","line":446,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L446-L455","doc":"The same fact in the form the refined bound uses: for a NONEMPTY diagonal,\n`D c + c ≤ λ₁`. The hypothesis cannot be dropped — see the section note. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_le_rowLen_zero_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_le_rowLen_zero_sub","kind":"theorem","line":433,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L433-L444","doc":"`D c` cells on diagonal `c` reach column `D c - 1 + c`, which lies in row `0`.\nStated with truncated subtraction so it holds unconditionally: past the first row\nboth sides are `0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_area_bound_card","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_area_bound_card","kind":"theorem","line":390,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L390-L404","doc":"**The area constraint, against `n`.** Chaining the two:\n\n    2 a · D a + D a (D a + 1)  ≤  2 n.\n\nSolving that quadratic for `D a` is exactly the bound the quadrature uses,\n`D a ≤ √(a² + 2n) - a`, stated here without `Real.sqrt`. In the rescaled\nvariables of `wlambda_covariance_epic_M1_limit_closed_form.py` this is the\n`A = 1` line — the fully deterministic one, needing no limit-shape input at all,\nwhich on its own already takes the budget from failing to a 0.094 % margin. The\n7.54 % figure quoted there uses `A_σ → n/2` instead, and that is a limit-shape\ninput, not this theorem. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_diagLen_le_card","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.sum_diagLen_le_card","kind":"theorem","line":367,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L367-L388","doc":"The diagonals are disjoint and sit inside the diagram, so any initial run of\nthem accounts for at most all `n` cells. This is what turns\n`diagLen_area_bound`'s partial sum into the area constraint `... ≤ n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_area_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_area_bound","kind":"theorem","line":347,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L347-L365","doc":"**The area bound.** Combining the two, the diagonals up to `a + D a` already\naccount for `a * D a + D a (D a + 1)/2` cells. Since that sum is at most the\nnumber of cells on that side of the diagonal — at most `n` — solving for `D a`\ngives `D a ≤ √(a² + 2A) - a`, the form used in the quadrature. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.triangle_le_sum_Ico","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.triangle_le_sum_Ico","kind":"theorem","line":328,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L328-L345","doc":"**1-Lipschitz tail piece.** Past `a` the profile falls by at most one per\nstep, so the next `D a` diagonals sum to at least the triangle `D a (D a + 1)/2`\n— stated doubled to stay in `ℕ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.mul_diagLen_le_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.mul_diagLen_le_sum","kind":"theorem","line":318,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L318-L326","doc":"**Monotonicity piece.** The first `a` diagonals are each at least as long as\nthe `a`-th, so they sum to at least `a * D a`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.abs_diagLen_sub_transpose_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.abs_diagLen_sub_transpose_le","kind":"theorem","line":254,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L254-L264","doc":"**Opposite-sign edges.** The two sides of the diagonal telescope to the same\ntotal `D 0`, which cancels; what is left is a difference of two drop-site\ncounts, each in `[0, D 0]`. So `|D_μ u − D_μᵀ v| ≤ D 0`, hence the slope is at\nmost `D 0 / g` where `g` is the content gap. This bound is sharp. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_transpose_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_transpose_zero","kind":"theorem","line":234,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L234-L252","doc":"The main diagonal is transpose-invariant — it is cut out by `(i, i) ∈ μ`,\nand the range bounds are implied by membership rather than extra conditions. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_sub_nonneg_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_sub_nonneg_le","kind":"theorem","line":224,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L224-L232","doc":"**Same-sign edges: the slope is a density.** For `u ≤ v` on one side of the\nmain diagonal, `D u − D v` counts the drop sites in `[u, v)`, so it lies between\n`0` and `v − u`; equivalently `(D u − D v)/(v − u) ∈ [0, 1]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_le_add_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_le_add_sub","kind":"theorem","line":214,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L214-L222","doc":"**Telescoped Lipschitz.** Over `v − u` steps the profile falls by at most\n`v − u`, since each single step falls by at most one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_le_diagLen_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_le_diagLen_zero","kind":"theorem","line":210,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L210-L212","doc":"**The main diagonal is the longest.** `D c ≤ D 0` for every `c`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_antitone","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_antitone","kind":"theorem","line":206,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L206-L208","doc":"Diagonals only shrink outward. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_lipschitz","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_lipschitz","kind":"theorem","line":171,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L171-L177","doc":"The two halves together: `|D c − D (c+1)| ≤ 1`, stated over `ℤ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_le_succ_add_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_le_succ_add_one","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L136-L169","doc":"**The 1-Lipschitz lemma.** `D c ≤ D (c+1) + 1`: a diagonal carries at most\none cell more than the next one out.\n\nIf it carried two more, the indices `k = D (c+1)` and `k+1` would both lie on\ndiagonal `c` and not on `c+1`, forcing `rowLen k = k + c + 1` and\n`rowLen (k+1) = k + c + 2` — a strictly longer row below a shorter one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_succ_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen_succ_le","kind":"theorem","line":129,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L129-L134","doc":"Diagonals shrink outward: `D (c+1) ≤ D c`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.mem_diagIdx","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.mem_diagIdx","kind":"theorem","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L124-L127","doc":"Membership, in the form the Lipschitz proof uses. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagIdx_eq_range","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagIdx_eq_range","kind":"theorem","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L120-L122","doc":"Hence the diagonal's index set is the initial segment `range (D c)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagIdx_downward_closed","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagIdx_downward_closed","kind":"theorem","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L108-L118","doc":"The indices on a diagonal are downward closed: `rowLen` is antitone, so a\nsmaller row reaches at least as far. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagLen","kind":"def","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L105-L106","doc":"`D c` — the number of cells on diagonal `c ≥ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagIdx","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.diagIdx","kind":"def","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L100-L103","doc":"Row indices meeting diagonal `c ≥ 0`: those `i` with `(i, i + c) ∈ μ`.\nBounded by `μ.colLen 0`, which every nonempty row index satisfies. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.factA_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.factA_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L78-L94","doc":"The product vanishes exactly when the two contents have equal absolute\nvalue — i.e. the edge is `ω₃`-neutral. Recorded because it identifies which\nedges contribute nothing to `P`, and those are exactly the ones a domination\nargument may discard. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.factA_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.factA_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L73-L76","doc":"**Fact A.** `ΔΣ|c| · Δω₃ ≥ 0` on every edge, hence `P ≥ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.factA_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.M1EdgeCalculus.factA_factor","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/M1EdgeCalculus.lean#L66-L71","doc":"The product of the two measured increments, factored. True of any integers:\n`c² = |c|²` is all it uses. ","signatureChunk":0}]}